Cervical cancer deaths in the UK have fallen by 75% since the introduction of the national screening programme in 1988 — one of the clearest demonstrations of screening effectiveness.
Childhood immunisation programmes have reduced diphtheria, tetanus, measles, and Hib disease by over 99% in countries with high coverage — conditions that killed hundreds of thousands annually before vaccination.
Finland's North Karelia Project (1972–1997) reduced cardiovascular mortality by 82% through community-based risk factor modification — diet, smoking, blood pressure — without new medical technologies.
